{
  "id": "DrugBank_DB11110 - MONDO_1567",
  "verbalization": "Magnesium Citrate is associated with Nephrocalcinosis through a chain of indications that all lead to hypercalcemia, a condition characterized by elevated calcium levels in the blood. This hypercalcemia is then linked to various calcium salts, including Calcium Acetate, Calcium Glucoheptonate, and Calcium Glycerophosphate, which are all implicated in the development of Nephrocalcinosis. The evidence suggests that Magnesium Citrate may contribute to Nephrocalcinosis by promoting hypercalcemia, which in turn increases the risk of calcium deposition in the kidneys. The shared pathway from Magnesium Citrate to hypercalcemia and then to Nephrocalcinosis highlights a mechanistic link that is consistent across different calcium salts. The integration of these pathways underscores the role of hypercalcemia in the pathogenesis of Nephrocalcinosis, with Magnesium Citrate acting as a potential mediator in this process.",
